package com.kaycee.meritprep

/**
 * JAMB UTME Chemistry syllabus map.
 * Topic headings follow the Chemistry PDF served by JAMB IBASS.
 * Content status is draft until independently reviewed by a qualified Chemistry teacher.
 */
object ChemistryCourse {
    const val SYLLABUS_VERSION = "JAMB-IBASS-2026.1"
    const val SOURCE_URL = "https://ibass.jamb.gov.ng/static/media/Chemistry.9efd0b98043d56922aaa.pdf"

    private fun mapped(
        id: String,
        title: String,
        objective: String,
        scope: String
    ) = LearningTopic(
        id = id,
        title = title,
        subjectCode = "CHEM",
        introduction = "This topic is part of the JAMB UTME Chemistry syllabus. Begin with the key idea, connect it to familiar materials and everyday processes, then practise applying the principle to unfamiliar examples.",
        objectives = listOf(objective),
        sections = listOf(
            "Syllabus scope" to scope,
            "How to study this topic" to "Define the key terms in your own words, explain the underlying particle-level or reaction-level process, work through numerical or symbolic examples where relevant, and practise interpreting observations, tables and graphs. The full lesson, worked examples and independent academic review are still being developed."
        ),
        checkQuestion = "What is the most useful first step when studying this topic?",
        checkOptions = listOf("Memorise isolated facts without context", "Understand the key terms and the principle behind them", "Skip examples and go straight to difficult questions", "Assume every substance behaves in the same way"),
        correctOption = 1,
        explanation = "Understanding the terms and governing principle makes it easier to explain observations and solve new problems.",
        isSyllabusOutline = true
    )

    val topics: List<LearningTopic> = listOf(
        LearningTopic(
            "chem-separation", "Separation of mixtures and purification", "CHEM",
            "A glass of muddy water, a sachet of salt and a bottle of petrol are mixtures or products that can be separated or purified in different ways. Chemistry teaches us to choose a method by looking at the physical properties that differ between the substances. The central question is not simply which apparatus to use, but which property makes separation possible.",
            listOf("Distinguish elements, compounds and mixtures", "Separate physical changes from chemical changes", "Explain purity using melting and boiling points", "Choose a separation method from the properties of a mixture"),
            listOf(
                "First identify what you have" to "An element contains one type of atom. A compound contains two or more elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio. A mixture contains substances physically combined in variable proportions; its components retain their chemical identities and can often be separated physically. Air is a mixture, pure water is a compound, and copper is an element.",
                "Physical change or chemical change?" to "A physical change alters state, shape or appearance without creating a new substance. Melting ice and dissolving common salt in water are physical changes. A chemical change produces new substances with different compositions, as when iron rusts or magnesium burns. A change of state alone does not change the chemical identity of a substance.",
                "Purity and characteristic temperatures" to "A pure substance has a characteristic melting point and boiling point at a specified pressure. An impurity often changes the melting point and broadens the melting range; it may also raise or lower the boiling temperature depending on the system. A temperature plateau during a phase change can therefore help assess purity, provided pressure and measurement conditions are controlled.",
                "Choose a method by the property that differs" to "Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a fluid by particle size. Decantation uses settling and careful pouring. Evaporation removes a volatile solvent to recover a dissolved solid. Crystallisation forms purer crystals from a solution. Simple distillation separates a solvent from dissolved substances or liquids with widely different boiling points; fractional distillation is used for miscible liquids with closer boiling points. A separating funnel works for immiscible liquids of different densities. Sublimation is useful when one solid changes directly to vapour. Chromatography separates substances by different affinities for a stationary phase and a moving solvent. Magnetisation separates magnetic material from non-magnetic material.",
                "Worked choice: salt and sand" to "Salt dissolves in water but sand does not. Add water and stir so the salt dissolves; filter the mixture to retain sand; then evaporate some water and allow the concentrated solution to crystallise. The filter paper separates sand by insolubility, while crystallisation recovers the dissolved salt. Dry the products separately.",
                "Common errors to avoid" to "Filtration does not remove dissolved salt from water. Evaporation to dryness may recover a solid but can damage heat-sensitive substances. Distillation depends on volatility and boiling behaviour, not simply on the size of particles. Chromatography is not just filtration: separation depends on differential movement between phases."
            ),
            "Which method is most suitable for separating insoluble sand from a salt solution?",
            listOf("Filtration", "Fractional distillation", "Paper chromatography", "Sublimation"), 0,
            "Sand is insoluble and remains as a solid on the filter paper while the salt solution passes through."
        ),
        LearningTopic(
            "chem-combination", "Chemical combination and the mole concept", "CHEM",
            "Chemical equations are a language for describing how substances react. To use that language accurately, chemists rely on measurements and a small set of laws that explain how mass and elements are conserved. This lesson builds from symbols and formulae to balanced equations, relative masses and the mole.",
            listOf("State the laws of chemical combination", "Interpret chemical symbols, formulae and equations", "Explain relative atomic mass on the carbon-12 scale", "Use the mole concept in simple stoichiometric calculations"),
            listOf(
                "The laws behind chemical formulae" to "The law of conservation of mass states that mass is conserved in a chemical reaction in a closed system. The law of definite proportions states that a pure compound contains the same elements in a fixed mass ratio. The law of multiple proportions compares the different simple whole-number mass ratios in which two elements can combine to form more than one compound. These laws help explain why formulae and balanced equations have definite ratios.",
                "Symbols, formulae and equations" to "A chemical symbol represents an element. A formula shows which atoms are present and their simplest or actual ratio in a substance. An equation represents a reaction: reactants are written on the left and products on the right. Coefficients balance the number of atoms; subscripts are part of the formula and must not be changed to balance an equation. For example, 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O has four hydrogen atoms and two oxygen atoms on each side.",
                "Relative atomic mass and the mole" to "Relative atomic mass (Aᵣ) compares the average mass of an atom of an element with one-twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom. Relative formula or molecular mass is found by adding the relative atomic masses in the formula. One mole contains the Avogadro number of specified entities, approximately 6.022 × 10²³. The molar mass in g mol⁻¹ is numerically equal to the relative formula or molecular mass.",
                "A first mole calculation" to "The relationship is n = m ÷ M, where n is amount in moles, m is mass in grams and M is molar mass in g mol⁻¹. For 18 g of water, M(H₂O) = (2 × 1) + 16 = 18 g mol⁻¹, so n = 18 ÷ 18 = 1 mol. Always write the formula, substitute values with units and check that the final unit is mol.",
                "Stoichiometry: use the balanced equation" to "The coefficients in a balanced equation give mole ratios, not mass ratios. For 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, two moles of hydrogen react with one mole of oxygen to produce two moles of water. Convert a known mass to moles, use the coefficient ratio, then convert the required amount back to mass if needed."
            ),
            "How many moles are present in 18 g of water? (H = 1, O = 16)",
            listOf("0.5 mol", "1.0 mol", "2.0 mol", "18 mol"), 1,
            "M(H₂O) = 18 g mol⁻¹; n = m ÷ M = 18 ÷ 18 = 1 mol."
        ),
        LearningTopic(
            "chem-kinetic-gas", "Kinetic theory of matter and gas laws", "CHEM",
            "A perfume smell spreads across a room because particles move. A tyre becomes harder when heated because gas particles move faster and collide more forcefully. Kinetic theory connects these familiar observations to the behaviour of particles and provides the foundation for gas-law calculations.",
            listOf("Describe solids, liquids and gases using particle motion", "Explain melting, boiling, evaporation, freezing and condensation", "State and apply Boyle's, Charles's and other gas laws", "Use PV = nRT in simple calculations and interpret gas-law graphs"),
            listOf(
                "The particle model" to "Matter is made of tiny particles in continuous motion. In a solid, particles are close together and vibrate about fixed positions. In a liquid, particles remain close but can move past one another. In a gas, particles are far apart and move rapidly and randomly. The model explains why gases are compressible and why liquids flow while retaining a nearly fixed volume.",
                "Changes of state" to "Heating generally increases the average kinetic energy of particles. During melting or boiling of a pure substance at constant pressure, added energy is used to overcome intermolecular attractions, so the temperature remains constant until the change is complete. Freezing and condensation release energy to the surroundings. Evaporation can occur at the surface below the boiling point; boiling occurs throughout the liquid when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure.",
                "Gas laws: keep track of what is constant" to "Boyle's law: at constant temperature and amount, pressure is inversely proportional to volume (P₁V₁ = P₂V₂). Charles's law: at constant pressure and amount, volume is directly proportional to absolute temperature (V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂). The pressure law: at constant volume and amount, pressure is directly proportional to absolute temperature (P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂). Always convert Celsius to kelvin using K = °C + 273.15 before using gas laws.",
                "The ideal gas equation" to "PV = nRT combines the relationships among pressure, volume, amount and absolute temperature. Use a consistent set of units for R, pressure and volume. An ideal gas is a model whose particles have negligible volume and no intermolecular attraction; real gases approach ideal behaviour at relatively low pressure and high temperature. Real gases deviate more at high pressure and low temperature.",
                "Worked example: Boyle's law" to "A gas occupies 600 cm³ at 100 kPa. If its temperature remains constant and the pressure increases to 150 kPa, P₁V₁ = P₂V₂ gives V₂ = (100 × 600) ÷ 150 = 400 cm³. The volume decreases because pressure and volume are inversely related at constant temperature."
            ),
            "Which temperature scale must be used in the combined gas law?",
            listOf("Celsius", "Fahrenheit", "Kelvin", "Any scale gives the same result"), 2,
            "Gas-law proportionalities require absolute temperature, measured in kelvin."
        ),
        LearningTopic(
            "chem-atomic-bonding", "Atomic structure, periodicity and chemical bonding", "CHEM",
            "Why does sodium react vigorously with water while neon is almost unreactive? Why does solid sodium chloride behave differently from copper metal? The answers begin with atomic structure, electron arrangement and the forces that hold particles together.",
            listOf("Distinguish atoms, molecules and ions", "Determine protons, neutrons and electrons from atomic and mass numbers", "Write electron configurations for the first 20 elements", "Explain periodic trends and compare ionic, covalent and metallic bonding"),
            listOf(
                "Inside the atom" to "An atom contains a small, dense nucleus of protons and neutrons, with electrons occupying regions around it. The atomic number Z is the number of protons. The mass number A is the total number of protons and neutrons, so neutrons = A − Z. A neutral atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons. Ions form when atoms or groups of atoms gain or lose electrons; the nucleus does not change during ordinary ion formation.",
                "Isotopes and electron arrangement" to "Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same proton number but different neutron numbers. Electrons occupy shells and subshells. For the first 20 elements, the introductory shell pattern is commonly represented as 2, 8, 8, 2, while subshell notation gives configurations such as sodium 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹. Do not confuse mass number with relative atomic mass, which is an abundance-weighted average for a natural sample.",
                "The periodic table" to "Elements are arranged in order of increasing atomic number. Elements in the same group have related outer-electron configurations and often similar chemical properties. Across a period, effective nuclear attraction generally increases and atomic radius tends to decrease. Down a group, additional electron shells generally increase atomic radius. Ionisation energy and electronegativity show broad periodic trends, with known exceptions that should be learned from evidence rather than treated as absolute rules.",
                "Why atoms bond" to "Atoms form chemical bonds because the resulting arrangement can be more stable. Ionic bonding involves electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions, commonly formed by electron transfer. Covalent bonding involves shared pairs of electrons. Metallic bonding is the attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons. Hydrogen bonding and van der Waals forces are intermolecular attractions and help explain physical properties such as boiling point.",
                "Molecular shape and properties" to "Electron-pair repulsion helps predict simple molecular shapes. Carbon dioxide is linear, water is bent, methane is tetrahedral and ammonia is trigonal pyramidal. Bonding and structure explain properties: many ionic solids have high melting points and conduct electricity when molten or dissolved; simple molecular substances often have lower melting points; metals conduct electricity because of mobile electrons."
            ),
            "How many neutrons are in an atom with mass number 23 and atomic number 11?",
            listOf("11", "12", "23", "34"), 1,
            "Neutrons = mass number − atomic number = 23 − 11 = 12."
        ),
        LearningTopic("chem-nuclear", "Nuclear chemistry", "CHEM",
            "Nuclear chemistry studies changes in the atomic nucleus. A smoke detector, a hospital imaging department and a nuclear power station may all involve radiation, but they use different sources and processes. Nuclear changes can release far more energy per atom than ordinary chemical reactions.",
            listOf("Distinguish nuclear reactions from ordinary chemical reactions", "Compare alpha, beta and gamma radiation", "Write simple nuclear equations using conservation of mass number and atomic number", "Calculate half-life and describe fission, fusion and uses of radioisotopes"),
            listOf(
                "The nucleus and radioactivity" to "The nucleus contains protons and neutrons, collectively called nucleons. Some combinations are unstable and change spontaneously by radioactive decay. Activity is measured in becquerels (Bq); 1 Bq means one nuclear decay per second. Decay rates of a large sample are not normally changed by temperature, pressure or chemical state.",
                "Three common types of radiation" to "An alpha particle is a helium nucleus with two protons and two neutrons. It has charge +2, high ionising power and low penetration; paper can stop it, but alpha emitters are hazardous if inhaled or swallowed. A beta-minus particle is a fast electron emitted when a neutron changes into a proton. It has charge −1 and moderate penetration; suitable plastic or thin aluminium can reduce it. Gamma radiation is high-energy electromagnetic radiation with no mass or charge. It is highly penetrating and is reduced by thick lead or concrete. Actual shielding depends on radiation energy and exposure conditions.",
                "Balancing nuclear equations" to "In a nuclear equation, total mass number (top number) and atomic number (bottom number) are conserved. Alpha decay reduces mass number by 4 and atomic number by 2. Beta-minus decay leaves mass number unchanged and increases atomic number by 1. Gamma emission changes neither. Example: ²³⁸₉₂U → ²³⁴₉₀Th + ⁴₂He. Check: 238 = 234 + 4 and 92 = 90 + 2.",
                "Half-life: worked example" to "Half-life is the time required for the number of undecayed nuclei, or activity, to fall to half its initial value. A sample starts at 800 counts per minute and has a half-life of 5 days. After 15 days, three half-lives have passed: 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 counts per minute. The remaining fraction is (1/2)³ = 1/8. In an experiment, correct detector readings for background radiation.",
                "Fission, fusion and applications" to "Nuclear fission splits a heavy nucleus into smaller nuclei, usually after neutron absorption, releasing energy and more neutrons. A controlled chain reaction can generate heat in a reactor. Nuclear fusion joins light nuclei and powers stars; it requires extremely high temperature and pressure. Radioisotopes are used in selected medical, industrial and research applications. Their use requires justification, shielding, monitoring, secure storage and appropriate disposal."
            ),
            "A sample has an activity of 640 Bq and a half-life of 2 hours. What activity is expected after 6 hours, assuming background has been corrected?",
            listOf("320 Bq", "160 Bq", "80 Bq", "40 Bq"), 2,
            "Six hours represents three half-lives: 640 → 320 → 160 → 80 Bq. The activity is 80 Bq."),
        LearningTopic("chem-solubility", "Solubility", "CHEM",
            "When salt disappears in water, it has not vanished. Its particles have spread among water molecules to form a solution. If more salt is added, a point is reached where no more dissolves under the same conditions. This limit helps explain crystallisation and many laboratory procedures.",
            listOf("Define solute, solvent, solution and solubility", "Distinguish unsaturated, saturated and supersaturated solutions", "Interpret solubility curves and make simple calculations", "Compare true solutions, colloids and suspensions"),
            listOf(
                "What happens during dissolving?" to "A solution is a homogeneous mixture. The solute is the substance being dissolved and the solvent is the medium that dissolves it. In salt water, sodium chloride is the solute and water is the solvent. Solute particles become dispersed among solvent particles. Solutions may be solid, liquid or gaseous; alloys are solid solutions.",
                "Solubility and saturation" to "Solubility is the maximum amount of a specified solute that dissolves in a stated amount of solvent at a specified temperature and, for gases, pressure to form a saturated solution. An unsaturated solution contains less than this maximum and can dissolve more. A saturated solution is at the equilibrium limit. A supersaturated solution contains more dissolved solute than is normally stable at that temperature; it is metastable and may crystallise if disturbed or seeded.",
                "Temperature, pressure and solubility curves" to "For many solid solutes, solubility increases with temperature, although exceptions exist. A solubility curve plots the mass of solute that dissolves in a fixed mass of solvent, often 100 g of water, against temperature. A point below the curve is unsaturated; a point on it is saturated; a point above it represents a supersaturated composition if the solute remains dissolved. Gas solubility in liquids generally increases with pressure and decreases as temperature rises.",
                "Worked classification" to "Suppose a salt has a solubility of 36 g per 100 g of water at a stated temperature. A solution containing 18 g per 100 g water is unsaturated; 36 g is saturated. If 45 g remains dissolved after careful cooling of a hot saturated solution, it is supersaturated. Always state the temperature and solvent mass when quoting solubility.",
                "Solution, colloid or suspension?" to "A true solution has molecular or ionic particles, does not settle and does not strongly scatter a beam of light. A colloid has intermediate-sized dispersed particles and may show the Tyndall effect; it does not readily settle. A suspension contains larger particles that may settle and can often be separated by ordinary filtration. Examples are salt solution, milk and muddy water, respectively."
            ),
            "A salt has a solubility of 40 g per 100 g water at 60 °C. A solution contains 25 g in 100 g water at 60 °C. How is it classified?",
            listOf("Saturated", "Unsaturated", "Supersaturated", "A suspension"), 1,
            "It contains less than the 40 g solubility limit at 60 °C, so it is unsaturated."),
        LearningTopic("chem-pollution", "Environmental pollution", "CHEM",
            "Streams and soils may receive oil, detergents, plastics, sewage and farm runoff. These materials can change water chemistry, reduce oxygen available to aquatic organisms and expose people to harmful substances. Environmental chemistry identifies pollutants, explains their effects and guides prevention.",
            listOf("Identify major air, water and soil pollutants and their sources", "Explain the effects of common pollutants", "Describe eutrophication, acid deposition and greenhouse warming", "Select practical prevention, treatment and monitoring measures"),
            listOf(
                "Air composition and air pollutants" to "Clean, dry air near sea level is mainly nitrogen (about 78%) and oxygen (about 21%), with argon and other gases making up most of the remainder; carbon dioxide is present at a much smaller concentration. Carbon monoxide forms during incomplete combustion and reduces the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity. Sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides irritate airways and contribute to acid deposition. Particulate matter can penetrate the respiratory system. Ground-level ozone forms through reactions involving nitrogen oxides and volatile organic compounds in sunlight; it is different from protective stratospheric ozone.",
                "Water pollution and oxygen demand" to "Sewage and organic waste introduce biodegradable material into water. Microorganisms decompose it and consume dissolved oxygen. Biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) is an operational measure of oxygen used by microorganisms to break down biodegradable organic matter under specified test conditions; high BOD can indicate heavy organic pollution. Oil can harm organisms and interfere with oxygen transfer. Toxic metals such as mercury, lead and cadmium can accumulate in food chains. Farm runoff may carry fertilisers and pesticides into waterways.",
                "Eutrophication and acid deposition" to "Excess nitrate and phosphate from fertilisers or sewage can stimulate rapid algal growth. When algae die, decomposition increases oxygen demand and may cause oxygen depletion, fish deaths and biodiversity loss. Sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides can react in the atmosphere to form acidic compounds that return in rain, fog or dry deposition. Acid deposition can acidify lakes and soils, damage vegetation and corrode buildings. Effects depend on emissions, weather and environmental buffering.",
                "Soil pollution and waste" to "Soil can be contaminated by poorly managed waste, oil spills, excessive agrochemicals, mining residues and industrial discharge. Persistent pollutants may harm soil organisms or enter crops and groundwater. Biodegradable materials can be broken down by organisms, but excessive waste can still create pollution. Many plastics persist for long periods. The waste hierarchy prioritises prevention and reduction, then reuse and recycling, followed by safe treatment and disposal.",
                "Prevention, treatment and monitoring" to "Control pollution at source: maintain engines, prevent oil leaks, apply fertilisers according to soil needs and keep untreated sewage out of waterways. Wastewater treatment commonly uses screening and settling, biological treatment to reduce organic load, and further treatment or disinfection where required. Monitoring may include pH, dissolved oxygen, BOD, turbidity, nutrients and selected toxic substances. Interpret results with sampling location, timing and method."
            ),
            "A lake receives fertiliser-rich runoff. Algal growth increases, then many fish die after the algae decay. What is the most direct cause?",
            listOf("A sudden increase in dissolved oxygen", "Oxygen depletion caused by microbial decomposition", "Conversion of all water into acid", "Removal of nitrogen from the atmosphere"), 1,
            "Decomposers use dissolved oxygen while breaking down dead algae. Severe oxygen depletion can suffocate fish."),
        LearningTopic("chem-acids-bases", "Acids, bases and salts", "CHEM",
            "Chemists describe acids and bases through the ions they produce or accept, and use indicators, pH measurements and titration to compare them. This topic connects familiar substances to the quantitative chemistry of neutralisation. Taste and touch are never safe laboratory tests.",
            listOf("Describe acids and bases using aqueous-ion and Brønsted–Lowry ideas", "Distinguish strong from weak acids and bases", "Use pH and pOH relationships for simple calculations", "Explain salt preparation, indicators, titration and buffer action"),
            listOf(
                "Acids and bases" to "In aqueous solution, an Arrhenius acid increases hydrogen-ion concentration (more accurately, hydronium ions, H₃O⁺), while an Arrhenius base increases hydroxide-ion concentration. In the Brønsted–Lowry model, an acid donates a proton and a base accepts one. HCl donates a proton to water: HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻. Ammonia accepts a proton: NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻. An alkali is a soluble base; not every base is an alkali.",
                "Strong and weak are not concentrated and dilute" to "A strong acid ionises almost completely in water; a weak acid ionises only partially. Strength describes extent of ionisation, not amount per volume. A dilute solution contains relatively little solute per volume; a concentrated solution contains relatively much. A dilute strong acid remains strong, and a concentrated weak acid remains weak. Acid basicity refers to the number of ionisable hydrogen ions per molecule in the reaction, not simply the number of hydrogen atoms in its formula.",
                "pH, pOH and indicators" to "For dilute aqueous solutions near 25 °C, pH = −log₁₀[H₃O⁺] and pOH = −log₁₀[OH⁻], with pH + pOH ≈ 14. A pH below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral and above 7 is alkaline under these conditions. Each pH unit represents a tenfold change in hydronium concentration. Litmus distinguishes acidic and alkaline conditions; methyl orange changes from red in acid through orange to yellow in alkali; phenolphthalein is colourless in acid and pink in alkaline solution. A calibrated pH meter gives a more quantitative reading.",
                "Neutralisation and salt preparation" to "An acid reacts with a base to form a salt and, for hydroxide bases, water. The net ionic equation for a strong acid reacting with a strong alkali is H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. A soluble salt can often be prepared by titrating an acid with a soluble alkali, repeating the measured reaction without indicator, then concentrating and crystallising the solution. An insoluble salt can be prepared by precipitation: mix suitable solutions, filter the precipitate, wash and dry it. The method depends on the required salt's solubility.",
                "Titration and buffers" to "Acid–base titration uses a measured volume of one solution to determine another solution's concentration. Fill a burette with titrant, pipette a measured analyte volume into a flask, add a suitable indicator and titrate while swirling to a persistent endpoint colour. Repeat for concordant titres. Use the balanced equation and mole ratio; do not assume every acid reacts in a 1:1 ratio. A buffer resists large pH changes when small amounts of acid or base are added. An acidic buffer commonly contains a weak acid and its conjugate base, such as ethanoic acid and sodium ethanoate."
            ),
            "Which statement correctly distinguishes acid strength from concentration?",
            listOf("A strong acid must always be concentrated", "A dilute acid is always weak", "Strength describes ionisation; concentration describes amount per unit volume", "Strength and concentration mean exactly the same thing"), 2,
            "Strength refers to extent of ionisation in water, while concentration refers to amount of solute in a given volume."),
        LearningTopic("chem-redox", "Oxidation and reduction", "CHEM",
            "When iron rusts, magnesium burns or a battery delivers current, electrons are transferred or redistributed. Oxidation and reduction provide a way to track these changes. The key is to recognise that the two processes occur together: when one species loses electrons, another gains them.",
            listOf("Define oxidation and reduction using oxygen, hydrogen and electron transfer", "Assign oxidation numbers in simple compounds and ions", "Identify oxidising and reducing agents", "Recognise and balance simple redox changes"),
            listOf(
                "Three useful definitions" to "Oxidation can mean gain of oxygen, loss of hydrogen, loss of electrons or an increase in oxidation number. Reduction can mean loss of oxygen, gain of hydrogen, gain of electrons or a decrease in oxidation number. These definitions describe the same broad chemical change from different perspectives. Oxidation and reduction always occur together in a redox reaction.",
                "Oxidation numbers" to "An oxidation number is a bookkeeping value assigned to an atom using agreed rules. An element in its uncombined form has oxidation number zero. A monatomic ion has an oxidation number equal to its charge. Oxygen is usually −2 in compounds, hydrogen is usually +1 when bonded to non-metals, and the sum of oxidation numbers equals the overall charge. These are common rules with exceptions, such as peroxides and metal hydrides. In neutral H₂O, 2(+1) + (−2) = 0.",
                "Identify what is oxidised and reduced" to "In Mg + Cu²⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Cu, magnesium changes from 0 to +2 and loses two electrons; it is oxidised. Copper(II) changes from +2 to 0 and gains two electrons; it is reduced. The oxidising agent causes oxidation by accepting electrons and is itself reduced. The reducing agent causes reduction by donating electrons and is itself oxidised. Do not confuse the agent with the substance undergoing the named change.",
                "Half-equations and electron balance" to "A half-equation shows the electron transfer for one part of a redox reaction. Oxidation: Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻. Reduction: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu. Adding the two equations cancels the electrons and gives Mg + Cu²⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Cu. In more complex equations, balance atoms first, then charge using electrons; in aqueous acidic or alkaline media, water and H⁺ or OH⁻ may also be needed.",
                "Worked oxidation-number example" to "In Fe₂O₃, oxygen is −2. The three oxygen atoms contribute −6 in total. Because the compound is neutral, the two iron atoms contribute +6, so each iron is +3. In FeO, iron is +2. When FeO is converted to Fe₂O₃, iron's oxidation number rises from +2 to +3, so iron is oxidised."
            ),
            "In the reaction Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, which species is the reducing agent?",
            listOf("Zn", "Cu²⁺", "Zn²⁺", "Cu"), 0,
            "Zinc loses electrons and is oxidised. It donates electrons to Cu²⁺, causing copper(II) to be reduced, so zinc is the reducing agent."),
        LearningTopic("chem-electrolysis", "Electrolysis", "CHEM",
            "A coating of chromium on a metal tap and the extraction of aluminium from its ore both depend on electrical energy driving chemical change. Electrolysis uses a direct electric current to cause a non-spontaneous reaction in an electrolyte. Understanding the ions present and the electrodes helps predict what forms.",
            listOf("Define electrolysis, electrolyte, electrode, anode and cathode", "Explain ion movement and electrode reactions", "Predict products for selected molten and aqueous electrolytes", "Describe electroplating, metal purification and factors affecting discharge"),
            listOf(
                "The electrolysis cell" to "An electrolyte is a molten ionic substance or a solution containing mobile ions that can conduct electricity. The cathode is the negative electrode in an electrolytic cell; positive ions (cations) move towards it and gain electrons (reduction). The anode is positive; negative ions (anions) move towards it and lose electrons (oxidation). Remember the electrode signs differ in a galvanic cell, so always identify the type of cell.",
                "Molten ionic compounds" to "A molten ionic compound contains only its own mobile ions, apart from impurities. During electrolysis of molten lead(II) bromide, Pb²⁺ ions move to the cathode and gain electrons: Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb. Bromide ions move to the anode and lose electrons: 2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻. The products are lead and bromine. No water is present to compete for discharge.",
                "Aqueous solutions and preferential discharge" to "In an aqueous electrolyte, water contributes H⁺ and OH⁻ ions, so products depend on the ions present, their relative ease of discharge, concentration and electrode material. At the cathode, a less reactive metal ion may be reduced to metal; otherwise hydrogen is often produced from water. At the anode, halide ions may be discharged in sufficiently concentrated solutions, while oxygen is commonly produced from hydroxide or water. The exact outcome depends on the electrolyte and conditions; learn standard syllabus examples rather than assuming one rule fits every solution.",
                "Electroplating and purification" to "In electroplating, the object to be coated is usually the cathode, the coating metal supplies ions (often as the anode), and the electrolyte contains ions of the coating metal. The object must be cleaned so the deposit adheres. In copper purification, impure copper is the anode, a thin sheet of pure copper is the cathode, and copper(II) sulfate solution is the electrolyte. Copper dissolves from the anode and deposits on the cathode; less reactive impurities may collect as anode sludge.",
                "Charge and Faraday's idea" to "The amount of substance formed at an electrode depends on the total electric charge passed. Charge Q = I × t, where I is current in amperes and t is time in seconds; the unit is coulomb. For the same substance and conditions, doubling the charge passed doubles the amount deposited. More detailed calculations use the Faraday constant and the number of electrons in the electrode reaction."
            ),
            "During electrolysis, which process occurs at the cathode?",
            listOf("Reduction: cations gain electrons", "Oxidation: anions lose electrons", "Neutralisation of the electrolyte", "Evaporation of the solvent"), 0,
            "The cathode is the site of reduction. Positive ions move towards it and gain electrons."),
        LearningTopic("chem-energy", "Energy changes", "CHEM",
            "Some reactions warm their surroundings, while others make a container feel cold. These observations show that chemical processes involve energy transfer. Chemists describe the heat transferred at constant pressure using enthalpy change and use energy diagrams to explain why reactions may release or absorb heat.",
            listOf("Distinguish exothermic and endothermic changes", "Explain energy changes using bond breaking and bond making", "Interpret simple enthalpy diagrams and signs of ΔH", "Apply simple calorimetry and Hess's law ideas"),
            listOf(
                "Exothermic and endothermic changes" to "An exothermic reaction transfers energy from the reacting system to the surroundings; the enthalpy change ΔH is negative. An endothermic reaction takes in energy from the surroundings; ΔH is positive. Combustion is usually exothermic. Thermal decomposition of calcium carbonate requires heat and is endothermic. The sign describes the system's enthalpy change, not whether the reaction is fast or slow.",
                "Bond breaking and bond making" to "Breaking chemical bonds requires energy; forming chemical bonds releases energy. The overall energy change depends on the difference between energy absorbed to break bonds and energy released when new bonds form. If bond formation releases more energy than bond breaking absorbs, the reaction is exothermic. If bond breaking requires more energy than bond formation releases, the reaction is endothermic. Do not say that breaking bonds releases energy.",
                "Enthalpy diagrams" to "In an exothermic energy profile, products lie at lower enthalpy than reactants and ΔH is negative. In an endothermic profile, products lie at higher enthalpy and ΔH is positive. Activation energy is the minimum energy barrier that reacting particles must overcome along the reaction pathway. A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy but does not change the enthalpy difference between reactants and products.",
                "Simple calorimetry" to "A simple calorimetry experiment estimates heat transferred by measuring temperature change in a known mass of water or solution. The heat gained or lost by the surroundings can be estimated using q = mcΔT, where m is mass, c is specific heat capacity and ΔT is temperature change. Use consistent units: for water, c is approximately 4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹. Heat loss to the surroundings, the container's heat capacity and incomplete reaction can cause error.",
                "Hess's law and energy cycles" to "Hess's law states that the enthalpy change for a reaction is independent of the route taken, provided the initial and final states are the same. This allows an unknown enthalpy change to be calculated from known steps in an energy cycle. Reverse an equation and change the sign of ΔH; multiply an equation and multiply ΔH by the same factor. Add equations algebraically and cancel substances appearing on both sides."
            ),
            "Which statement is correct for an exothermic reaction?",
            listOf("ΔH is negative and energy is transferred to the surroundings", "ΔH is positive and the system absorbs heat", "Products always have higher enthalpy than reactants", "The reaction must be instantaneous"), 0,
            "An exothermic process releases energy from the system to the surroundings, so the system's enthalpy change is negative."),
        LearningTopic("chem-rates", "Rates of chemical reaction", "CHEM",
            "A tablet reacts faster when crushed, and many reactions speed up when warmed. These observations can be explained by collision theory: particles must collide with enough energy and in a suitable orientation for a reaction to occur. Reaction rate describes how quickly reactants are used or products are formed.",
            listOf("Define reaction rate and interpret simple rate data", "Explain collision theory and activation energy", "Describe effects of temperature, concentration, pressure and surface area", "Explain the role of catalysts and interpret rate graphs"),
            listOf(
                "What is reaction rate?" to "Reaction rate is the change in concentration of a reactant or product per unit time. It may be estimated by measuring gas volume, mass change, colour intensity or concentration as a reaction proceeds. The average rate over an interval is change in measured quantity divided by elapsed time. Initial rate refers to the rate near the start of a reaction, where concentrations are known most accurately.",
                "Collision theory and activation energy" to "Particles must collide for a reaction to occur, but not every collision is successful. A successful collision has sufficient energy to overcome the activation-energy barrier and a suitable orientation. Increasing the number of successful collisions per second increases reaction rate. Activation energy is the minimum energy barrier for the reaction pathway; it is not the same as the overall enthalpy change.",
                "Concentration, pressure and surface area" to "Increasing the concentration of a dissolved reactant usually increases the frequency of collisions per unit volume. Increasing the pressure of a gaseous reactant at constant temperature increases the number of gas particles per unit volume and can increase collision frequency. Crushing a solid reactant increases its exposed surface area, allowing more collisions at the solid surface. These changes affect rate; they do not automatically change the final equilibrium position in every system.",
                "Temperature and catalysts" to "Raising temperature increases average kinetic energy and the fraction of particles with energy at or above activation energy. Therefore, a larger fraction of collisions can be successful. A catalyst increases reaction rate by providing an alternative pathway with lower activation energy. It is not used up overall and does not change the reaction's enthalpy change or equilibrium constant. Enzymes are biological catalysts with specific conditions and substrates.",
                "Investigating rate fairly" to "A rate experiment should change one independent variable while keeping other relevant variables constant. For example, compare equal masses of the same marble-chip size with the same acid volume and concentration at different temperatures. Record gas volume at fixed time intervals or measure mass loss on a balance. Repeat measurements, plot the data and compare initial slopes. Control chip size, apparatus leakage, temperature and measurement timing; explain how these limitations affect the result."
            ),
            "Why does powdered calcium carbonate usually react faster than the same mass of large chips with dilute acid?",
            listOf("Powder has a greater exposed surface area", "Powder has a lower formula mass", "Powder contains more calcium atoms", "The acid becomes more concentrated automatically"), 0,
            "Powder exposes more surface to the acid, increasing the frequency of effective collisions at the solid surface."),
        mapped("chem-equilibrium", "Chemical equilibrium", "Explain dynamic equilibrium and predict shifts using Le Chatelier's principle.", "Reversible reactions; equilibrium position; concentration, pressure and temperature effects; industrial applications; equilibrium constant concept."),
        mapped("chem-nonmetals", "Non-metals and their compounds", "Describe preparation, properties, tests and uses of selected non-metals and their compounds.", "Hydrogen; halogens and hydrogen chloride; oxygen and sulphur; oxides; ozone; water, hardness and treatment; relevant laboratory tests."),
        mapped("chem-metals", "Metals and their compounds", "Relate metal properties, extraction and reactivity to their uses.", "General properties; reactivity series; extraction of selected metals; iron, aluminium and copper; alloys; corrosion and prevention."),
